#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:39
#: b97d88df1ab84679bdc95764a35f6c7d
msgid "I2C"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:40
#: 459340ca2ef24f0f962a31e44ec304be
msgid "The I2C bus is widely used by autopilots to connect low-bandwidth peripheral components such as magnetometers (compasses), digital airspeed sensors, rangefinders, tachometers, and other sensors. Multiple devices can be connected to a single I2C bus, each identified by a unique address. I2C employs a two-wire open-drain communication method using Serial Clock (SCL) and Serial Data (SDA) lines. The bus lines are typically pulled up to a positive voltage (3.3V or 5V) through resistors, ensuring a default idle state at logic high."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:44
#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:74
#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:89
#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:104
#: 2d6510ebcfc5462d9f1a2eece938ddbc
#: 5eabfb32673c49808a91e3ccd4013eed
#: 1509da7b1e9d4e678e56af7a6320e37f
#: 1d23783a28534daead1c74ea0066486b
msgid "Cable Recommendations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:45
#: 0fe5421013f6417ab4a3e86f022abd2b
msgid "Proper cable handling is mandatory for reliable I2C communication, as inappropriately routed I2C signals could be prone to electromagnetic interference (EMI) and crosstalk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:47
#: 7c0cc8de8a8e4812a1a788a2758330f5
msgid "Keep cables as short as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:48
#: dd39fe94911c43f9a6dd7b18a74efe36
msgid "Use twisted pairs to reduce crosstalk: - Twist each pair (SCL/+5V and SDA/GND) about 10 turns per 30 cm. - Twist both pairs together about 4 turns per 30 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:51
#: 11f263272265447ca82c840fa0f47197
msgid "For larger vehicles or long cables, consider using CAN bus or other differential signaling interfaces instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:54
#: 8418aba95ae7466abb0f175937b154f1
msgid "Pull-up Resistors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:55
#: 70007dd8bec0487f8be3147931c91cd5
msgid "Pull-up resistors on both SDA and SCL lines are essential. Autopilots or peripheral devices typically have these resistors built-in. An oscilloscope measurement is sometimes required to check correct value of pull-up resistors. If the signal amplitude is too low, increase resistor values. If the signals are rounded, decrease resistor values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:58
#: 967630cd4faa480697db38019229eddc
msgid "Common Problems"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:59
#: 26c38d6d2ab140d0a55508207789331f
msgid "**Address Clashes**: Occur when multiple devices on the bus share the same address, preventing proper communication. Possible solutions include: - Changing the device's address via hardware or software configuration, if supported. - Use an address translator to remap address, such as from `ThunderFly <https://docs.thunderfly.cz/avionics/TFI2CADT01/>`__ or `Adafruit <https://www.adafruit.com/product/5914>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:63
#: 93a16abdfe974c669824fded31bfd8d5
msgid "**Excessive Wiring Capacitance**: Long cables or multiple devices connected to a single bus significantly increase cable capacitance, resulting in degraded signal quality. Possible solutions include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:65
#: e489954bf4334cf98322d30b69f82ad0
msgid "Reducing cable length or using higher-quality cables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:66
#: 47feb0a9575849029347d2d797521bef
msgid "Splitting devices across multiple I2C buses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:67
#: 134b72af2ba34ccb838e0f65fbcb0868
msgid "Use an I2C bus extender such as `Adafruit I2C Extender <https://www.adafruit.com/product/4756>`__ , or `ThundeFly's I2C bus accelerators or extenders <https://docs.thunderfly.cz/avionics/TFI2CEXT01/>`__ to boost signal quality over extended cable lengths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:70
#: 84aea029ce4b4c2eaaa4dab62684a3d7
msgid "CAN"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:71
#: 04e032c737f14ef9b2a2376b2540b8b9
msgid ":ref:`CAN <common-canbus-setup-advanced>` bus is provided on many autopilots for use with :ref:`DroneCAN <common-uavcan-setup-advanced>` peripherals. ESCs, GPS/Compass, Rangefinders, and many other peripherals and sensors are being added to the list of available DroneCAN devices everyday. CAN provides a robust method of communicating with peripherals with data integrity, even with long leads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:76
#: 36798cb931d3401db510abfd546ae4df
msgid "CAN cables should use twisted pairs to reduce electromagnetic interference (EMI) and maintain signal integrity on cables over 6 inches:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:78
#: 2e5e4026597f4b56ade652c2da76ee61
msgid "Twist each signal pair (CAN_H/CAN_L and +5V/GND) approximately 10 turns per 30 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:79
#: 9050ad97c7154ed2809ee9e310922383
msgid "Twist both pairs together about 4 turns per 30 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:80
#: 97c1a9bee0cd4d3d846fa0e095905a26
msgid "Maintain separation from high-power and high-noise cables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:82
#: 455ec5cf32a04bd7be45818c53c08f03
msgid "Due to its differential signaling and robust protocol, CAN is particularly suitable for applications requiring reliability over longer cable lengths and in electrically noisy environments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:85
#: c0a128863e104b5e8ee057da291b6de9
msgid "SPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:86
#: 585135868eeb4fd7b3a5ce6234720e93
msgid "SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) is a synchronous serial communication protocol used by autopilots to connect higher-bandwidth peripherals and sensors, such as optical flow sensors, specialized telemetry modems, barometers, IMUs, and other advanced digital sensors. It supports full-duplex communication using separate lines for data input and output. Most autopilots have processors with multiple SPI ports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:90
#: b78e710fcc1b4b7eb11937f574380b5a
msgid "SPI signals can be sensitive to electromagnetic interference (EMI) and crosstalk, especially at higher clock rates. To minimize these issues:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:92
#: 3690278efcf442c8b8bb9ff2dd84d816
msgid "Keep cable lengths as short as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:93
#: 75f6020999214bb98fc91536572a382a
msgid "Ensure signal cables are separated from high-power and noisy cables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:96
#: 0692c3bfb9904e0c8a331e04cdd82d74
msgid "UART"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:97
#: fc0f26ab09aa456ca297e57a48869316
msgid "UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter) ports are commonly provided by autopilots to connect peripherals such as telemetry radios, GPS receivers, rangefinders, radio modems, and even SBUS servos. Serial RC receiver inputs can be connected to any UART. See :ref:`Serial Port Configuration <common-serial-options>`. Usually, several UART ports are provided."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:99
#: 1bce46577dff45259246911b0c475e39
msgid "By default, UART connections are point-to-point and do not support bus-style networking. Each UART line directly connects two devices. The connecting cable is typically straight (1:1), meaning no crossing is performed within the cable itself. Therefore, the peripheral devices must internally cross RX/TX signals as required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:101
#: a6331ab365304787be9621c6632ca706
msgid "Some UART ports provide CTS (Clear To Send) and RTS (Request To Send) signals to allow  a hardware handshake mechanism that improves reliability by preventing data overflow. If the peripheral does not utilize these signals, they can typically remain disconnected. ArduPilot also provides a means to disable this flow control using the ``BRD_SERx_RTSCTS`` parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:105
#: 8f00fc5c4f3a4496babb6e54e76658dc
msgid "UART signals, particularly at higher baud rates, can generate electromagnetic interference (EMI). However, this is not normally an issue in most vehicles. However, to minimize EMI:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:107
#: 88174a264bbe4abaad5f122f31d19dff
msgid "Keep UART cable lengths as short as practical."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:108
#: bd2005aed1474f0abc38bac7ca8e1f3c
msgid "Separate UART cables from high-power wires and sensitive sensors, such as GPS receivers and magnetometers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-cabling-guide.rst:110
#: 992f6f9d905647afbaf9493ad1ec9b1b
msgid "Unlike twisted-pair cables used for differential protocols (CAN, I2C), cable twisting is generally not applicable for UART signals due to their single-ended nature."
msgstr ""
